From 8b84e0a2996dd28deae4410bcf00f59c86960dcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiucheng Zang <git.jiucheng@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 16:46:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Implement pinned Git skill import and the versioned registry

Closes #3.

Imports a skill as a complete directory from one exact revision. Only the
requested commit is fetched, into a temporary bare repository, and files are
read from the Git object database and written by Skim rather than checked out,
so nothing from the source repository ever lands on disk as-is. Files are
staged in a temporary directory and moved into skills/<slug>/ with a single
rename, so a rejected import leaves the managed repository untouched.

Rejects non-https/file URLs, short or non-hex commits, absolute, traversing,
and .git subdirectories, commits missing from the source, symbolic links,
submodules, nested Git directories, oversized directories, a missing SKILL.md,
invalid frontmatter, and an existing destination. A repeated import is
reported instead of overwriting what is already installed.

Reads the registry from the committed tree at the managed repository HEAD,
which becomes the shared configuration version. Skill records, per-file
SHA-256 hashes, scopes, and enabled state are always recomputed from that
tree, so two reads at the same HEAD return identical records and uncommitted
edits stay invisible until they are committed. .skim/registry.json is the
committed materialization and the provenance ledger that carries a skill's
source URL, commit, subdirectory, and license across reads.

Adds GET /api/registry, and a no-conflict fixture vendored from
anthropics/skills at 34040c9c568585f6929bedeaad110ad08f079624 with its Apache
2.0 license file and expected file hashes. An opt-in test
(SKIM_NETWORK_TESTS=1) imports that pinned commit over the network and checks
it against the vendored hashes.

Also negates the user-global lib/ ignore rule for src/lib, which was silently
keeping new application source out of Git.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
